WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Manual laundry once swallowed entire days of scrubbing. Electric motors and stamped galvanized

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:11.060
tubs cut the machine's price from weeks of wages down to a few days. This is First Principles

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:17.320
Daily, episode 73. Before the electric washing machine reached ordinary homes, a typical

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.160
load of laundry demanded hours of lifting, soaking, rubbing on a washboard, ringing and rinsing

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.120
by hand. The labor itself was the dominant cost, measured in human time rather than dollars.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:35.660
Once manufacturers applied electric motors to drive agitation and used volume stamping to form

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.960
galvanized steel tubs, the capital price of the machine itself fell sharply. That shift turned an

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:53.140
expensive specialized appliance into something a middle-income household could buy outright. For

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.720
washboards, wooden tubs, and hand-cranked ringers simply mechanized the same motions a person

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.500
performed at a river bank or in a wash tub. Designers copied the shapes and materials of existing

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.000
hand tools because those forms felt proven and required no new factories or skills. The finished

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.620
devices therefore carried heavy wooden frames, individually formed metal liners, and separate hand-powered

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:23.500
mechanisms that still needed constant human effort to operate. A typical early mechanical washer

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:29.320
might sell for a sum equal to several weeks of factory wages, yet most of that price reflected the

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.400
small batch fabrication of each tub, and the assembly labor needed to fit the parts together.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.480
No one questioned why the tub had to be shaped by hand, or why the agitator required a separate

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.380
crank for each cycle. The convention simply reproduced the steps of hand washing at larger scale.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:52.500
Supply chains stayed local and fragmented, with wooden staves sourced one coupe rage at a time

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.340
and metal liners beaten or rolled in small shops. Because each machine was essentially a custom

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.680
assembly of familiar components, the price remained high enough that only institutions or wealthy

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.120
households bought them. The assumption baked into the entire trade was that laundry would always

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.680
consume large amounts of time, so the equipment itself did not need to be cheap or simple to produce.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:20.780
That mindset kept the capital cost anchored far above the value of the steel, copper, and wood

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:26.740
inside the machine. Even when early power sources such as water wheels or steam engines appeared in

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:32.580
commercial laundries, the core geometry of the tub and the sequence of agitation stayed identical to

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:38.820
the hand version. The goal was to scale the known process rather than to ask what forces actually had

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.840
to act on the fabric. The result was a device whose every joint, seam and linkage carried the

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:50.140
accumulated labor of fitting parts that had never been designed to nest or align without adjustment.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.900
Owners accepted the high price and the ongoing maintenance because the alternative was still the

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:01.820
full day of manual work. The machine merely reduced the physical strain without changing the underlying

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:08.200
economics of production. Maytag and a handful of competitors began by asking what physical work

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:13.240
actually had to be performed on the clothes and what materials could deliver that work at the

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:19.320
lowest cost once electricity was available. The raw material floor for a basic household washer

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:26.060
consists of a steel tub, an electric motor, a simple agitator or impeller, a transmission, wiring,

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:32.420
and a supporting frame. Commodity steel sheet in the early 20th century traded at a few cents per pound.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.760
A galvanized tub might contain roughly 40 to 60 pounds of steel while the motor and drive added

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.580
perhaps another 20 pounds of copper and iron. Adding the smaller quantities of rubber, wood and

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.400
fasteners produced a magic wand estimate around 10 to 15 dollars in materials for an early electric

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.660
model, though exact commodity prices fluctuated with wartime demand and regional supply.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.940
Under the old hand-built approach, the finished machine sold for several times that figure, yielding

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:06.960
idiot index that signaled most of the price resided in fabrication steps rather than in the

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:13.000
atoms themselves. The first decisive change was the switch from wooden or riveted tubs to deep-drawn

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.240
galvanized steel, formed on presses that could turn out hundreds of identical shells per day.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.580
Stamping eliminated the skilled labor of fitting staves or welding seams and removed dozens of

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:30.360
leak paths that had required hand caulking. Next came the integration of a fractional horse power

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.780
electric motor directly coupled to a simple gear or belt drive, replacing the separate hand

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.900
crank and its associated castings. Because the motor could run at constant speed, designers could

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:48.060
replace multiple valve and linkage families with a single cam or timer sequence, cutting part count

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:54.120
and the hours needed to assemble and adjust each unit. Galvanizing the stamp tub in a single dip

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.080
further reduced corrosion worries and allowed thinner sheet without sacrificing durability.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.900
Each of these moves attacked a specific slice of the old idiot index. Stamping attacked forming

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:11.700
labor, the motor attacked human time input, and the simplified drive attacked the number of custom

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:17.480
machined pieces. The trade-off that had to be one was reliable electrical insulation and safe

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:23.320
starting torque. Early motors were therefore deliberately overbuilt with heavy copper windings until

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:29.400
insulation materials improved. Once those process problems were solved at scale, the same presses

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.660
and winding machines could serve many thousands of machines, spreading their capital costs across

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:41.140
volume. A natural objection is that electricity itself was not universally available, yet the

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:46.480
redesign still lowered the machine's price even in regions where power arrived later, because the

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.180
capital cost of the device itself had already been compressed. Another objection is that stamped

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:58.160
steel might dent or corrode faster than thick wood, but the galvanizing layer and the ability to replace

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.440
a damaged tub quickly offset those concerns for most buyers. The arithmetic becomes clearer when one

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:10.200
compares the labor hours. A Cooper might spend half a day shaping and fitting a single wooden tub

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:16.200
while the press produced the same volume of steel in minutes. That ratio repeated across thousands

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:22.560
of units is what pulled the finished price toward the material floor. By the late 1920s, a basic

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.640
electric washer from Maytag or its peers sold for a price equivalent to a few days of average wages

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:35.740
rather than weeks, an ownership spread rapidly into working households. The new idiot index sat far

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:41.300
lower than the hand-built predecessors because the dominant costs had moved from skilled assembly into

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:47.420
the repeatable operations of stamping and motor winding. Even so, the machine still required a separate

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:53.560
ringer, manual filling and draining, and regular maintenance of belts and seals, so the labor reduction

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:59.700
was only partial. The physical floor remained limited by the need for a pressure-tight tub, a reliable

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:06.000
motor, and enough structure to survive decades of vibration. No redesign could eliminate those elements

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.920
without changing the task itself. Later automatic models would attack the remaining manual steps,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:18.000
but the early electric galvanized machines already demonstrated how volume processes could compress the

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.480
gap between material value and finished price. One limit that persisted was the requirement for a separate

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:30.360
power source and water supply. In homes without indoor plumbing, the machine still demanded significant

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.480
setup labor, so the full benefit appeared only when paired with other household infrastructure

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:41.260
improvements. The idiot index could not drop to one because the motor windings, the gear reduction,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:47.360
and the final assembly still added value beyond the raw commodities, yet the index had already fallen

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:53.880
from the double or triple digits of the hand-built era into a much narrower band. A tub that once required

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:59.500
a Cooper and a Riveter to assemble now emerges from a single press stroke because the designer treated

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:05.460
the shape as a repeatable steel shell rather than a wooden barrel. An agitator that once needed

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:11.180
a separate crank for each load now receives its motion from a motor whose windings are laid down by

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:16.760
machine, showing that the cost lives in how the motion is produced, not in the motion itself.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:23.460
The same logic of asking what work must actually occur and which process can deliver it at the lowest

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:29.900
material multiple applies to any product whose price still reflects custom fitting instead of repeatable

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:35.520
forming. Tomorrow the show returns with another concrete case or an industry whose numbers still

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.820
sit far above their physical floor, which every day object whose price feels fixed would shift first

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:47.260
if someone rebuilt it from the atom's upward. That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

